184
00:38:51.360 --> 00:38:51.880
Okay.

185
00:38:51.880 --> 00:38:55.680
So I'll use my last two minutes
to talk about authorship

186
00:38:55.680 --> 00:38:58.680
a little bit.

187
00:38:59.280 --> 00:39:03.200
How is authorship distributed across
the making and readings of these worlds?

188
00:39:03.200 --> 00:39:08.040
So I'm going to talk about gathering,
composing, and navigating.

189
00:39:08.120 --> 00:39:12.160
So if you think about traditional

190
00:39:12.160 --> 00:39:16.320
collage— So Benesch has written
a book called “Collage Culture.”

191
00:39:16.320 --> 00:39:20.320
In traditional collage, meaning no longer
resides in a stable composition organized

192
00:39:20.320 --> 00:39:24.000
around a central point of view, but
emerges through relations among fragments.

193
00:39:24.080 --> 00:39:25.920
I think most people who are familiar
with collage

194
00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:27.680
have thought about these kinds of ideas.

195
00:39:27.680 --> 00:39:30.920
Collage unsettles
the idea of a unified subject position,

196
00:39:30.960 --> 00:39:33.240
suggesting
instead that perception and experience

197
00:39:33.240 --> 00:39:37.120
are assembled from partial, heterogeneous,
and often conflicting elements.

198
00:39:37.240 --> 00:39:42.280
So in this 3D version of collage
that participants make,

199
00:39:42.320 --> 00:39:45.720
they work with found photographs
and family photographs, Internet images,

200
00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:50.160
all kinds of materials, and the materials
are gathered through lived experience.

201
00:39:50.480 --> 00:39:54.280
So that's one thing that I think
makes this process unique

202
00:39:54.280 --> 00:39:56.040
compared
to more traditional forms of collage,

203
00:39:56.040 --> 00:39:59.240
is that most material
that people work with is coming

204
00:39:59.240 --> 00:40:02.400
from their own life and community.

205
00:40:02.920 --> 00:40:07.760
The archive that people are working with,
often they create during the project.

206
00:40:07.760 --> 00:40:09.640
They're not working with established
archives

207
00:40:09.640 --> 00:40:11.920
like a library or a newspaper in general.

208
00:40:11.920 --> 00:40:16.040
And sometimes the archive, for example,
like in the Nebraska Project, is

209
00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:20.560
collectively produced. So.

210
00:40:21.800 --> 00:40:24.000
Composing is a second phase.

211
00:40:24.000 --> 00:40:29.120
So in the process of composing,
you add an image, you walk around,

212
00:40:29.240 --> 00:40:31.880
you test it, you navigate it,
you scan something else,

213
00:40:31.880 --> 00:40:34.960
you change a scale, you rotate,
you revisit things.

214
00:40:34.960 --> 00:40:38.480
So during the composing process,
the author inhabits the world.

215
00:40:38.520 --> 00:40:41.520
The author is switching roles
between maker and viewer,

216
00:40:41.680 --> 00:40:46.320
and the author makes
and remakes an environment.

217
00:40:46.320 --> 00:40:50.640
So in a way, I would say that
the author in this case composes

218
00:40:50.640 --> 00:40:54.640
relations and meaning emerges
through process and exploration.

219
00:40:54.920 --> 00:41:00.600
And this relates to Ingold,
who says that making can be understood

220
00:41:00.600 --> 00:41:04.440
as a process of wayfinding, which form
and meaning arise through engagement

221
00:41:04.440 --> 00:41:08.000
in materials, rather through the execution
of a predefined plan.

222
00:41:08.000 --> 00:41:10.480
So one thing— I'm going to wrap up in
just a second.

223
00:41:10.480 --> 00:41:12.760
So one thing that I find
really interesting about this process

224
00:41:12.760 --> 00:41:17.200
is that the storytelling aspect
of these visual worlds is really emergent.

225
00:41:17.200 --> 00:41:20.240
So almost nobody has a preexisting plan.

226
00:41:20.240 --> 00:41:21.160
Or even if they do,

227
00:41:21.160 --> 00:41:24.680
they get led in different directions
as they compose the relationships

228
00:41:24.680 --> 00:41:29.880
between the objects in the 3D space
and then finally navigating.

229
00:41:29.880 --> 00:41:34.160
So if you're the viewer, you walk,
you turn your approach, you enter,

230
00:41:34.280 --> 00:41:38.680
and in the process, you might notice
and discover and compare and revisit.

231
00:41:38.840 --> 00:41:41.760
Narrative emerges through navigation.

232
00:41:41.760 --> 00:41:46.320
The author composes relationships
and the reader composes narratives.

233
00:41:46.320 --> 00:41:47.720
So it's almost as though the author is

234
00:41:48.960 --> 00:41:52.040
composing a space where

235
00:41:52.600 --> 00:41:56.400
relationships and associations
and meanings can occur.

236
00:41:56.400 --> 00:42:01.240
And then the reader kind of completes
this space by choosing their own path

237
00:42:02.120 --> 00:42:03.000
through this space.

238
